      <div class="home-head">
        <div class="home-eyebrow home-eyebrow--section">Architecture</div>
        <h2>One decision pipeline, two deployment shapes.</h2>
        <p>All detection logic lives in pure <code>*_rules.lua</code> modules with zero <code>ngx.*</code> dependencies. Thin adapters perform I/O, which keeps the rules unit-testable under a plain Lua interpreter and the edge behaviour deterministic.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="home-split">
        <div class="home-copy">
          <div class="panel">
            <h3>Request pipeline</h3>
            <pre><code>Client Request → Nginx access_by_lua_block
  → pattern_analyzer.analyze_request()
  → router.decide_route() (12 stages)
  → pool_router.get_or_assign_pool()
  → session_handler.update_session() → Redis
  → WordPress backend: production or honeypot
  → Suricata eve.json → init_worker.lua
  → threat_intel shared dict
  → abuseipdb_client.report_ip_async()</code></pre>
          </div>
          <ul class="home-points">
            <li><Icon name="check" class="w-4 h-4" /><span><strong>Pure / adapter split.</strong> Decisions live in rules modules; adapters talk to Redis, Nginx and AbuseIPDB.</span></li>
            <li><Icon name="check" class="w-4 h-4" /><span><strong>Graceful degradation.</strong> If Redis or a pool is unavailable, the router falls back instead of dropping customer traffic.</span></li>
            <li><Icon name="check" class="w-4 h-4" /><span><strong>Two hosts, one flow.</strong> The edge can ship telemetry to a separate SIEM host over mTLS, outbound only.</span></li>
          </ul>
        </div>

